Description: Write /etc/netplan/*.yaml atomically and durably
 NetworkManager's netplan integration rewrites /etc/netplan/90-NM-<uuid>.yaml on
 every activation (to persist connection.timestamp / seen-bssids) using an
 in-place fopen()/open(O_TRUNC)+write() with no temporary file and no fsync. If
 the boot is interrupted (power loss, reboot, SIGKILL) between the truncate and
 the data becoming durable, the file is left at 0 bytes, which breaks networking
 on the next boot.
 .
 Convert the three YAML writers (netplan_netdef_write_yaml,
 netplan_state_update_yaml_hierarchy and netplan_state_write_yaml_file) to the
 mkstemp + fsync + atomic rename idiom (plus a best-effort directory fsync),
 mirroring the temp+rename pattern netplan_state_write_yaml_file already used.
 The library already uses the same approach for NM keyfiles, so only the YAML
 emitter path was unsafe.
Author: Tim Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Forwarded: not-yet
Last-Update: 2026-06-26

